      "subtype": "flow",
      "slots": {
        "source": "self.poolmanager",
        "sink": "clear()"
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "The second step loops over `self.proxy_manager`, a dictionary mapping proxy URLs to urllib3 `ProxyManager` instances that the adapter created on demand when requests went through proxies, and calls `clear()` on each one so their pooled connections are closed as well.",
      "type": "dataflow",
      "subtype": "flow",
      "slots": {
        "source": "self.proxy_manager.values()",
        "sink": "proxy.clear()"
      }
    },
    {
      "text": "Note that this method does not reset the dictionary itself, so the proxy managers remain referenced by the adapter after closing, though their pools are emptied.",
      "type": "intent",
      "subtype": "other",
      "slots": {}
    },
    {
      "text": "In practice, `close` is invoked when a `Session` is closed, typically through a context manager or an explicit `session.close()` call, which ensures sockets are not left open after the work is done.",
